@prefix intention: <http://example.org/orexis/intention#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/intention#> .
@prefix desire: <http://example.org/orexis/desire#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/intention> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """BDI's third letter: a commitment to reduce a named gap by a named means, which persists until it is resolved rather than being re-decided on every sensing.

**An intention is an amortised deliberation.** The sharpest lesson in the BDI literature is that intentions exist to SAVE deliberation — an agent that re-decides on every reading is a reflex machine wearing a planner's name — and here that is a cost model rather than philosophy: deliberation is about to include an LLM call, and the commitment is what makes one affordable. This package is the substrate that makes that persistence a FACT with a lifecycle instead of a scatter of module state: `bidding.pending`, a held claim and a commanded cadence were all intentions before this package named them.

**Nothing here decides.** Deciding what to commit to is deliberation's — the welded reflex chain today, a family with an LLM member later. This package only KEEPS: it records an adoption, resolves it as satisfied or dropped with the reason, refuses to re-adopt what is already standing, and answers what stands. The seam between keeping and deciding is the whole reason they are two capabilities.

See knowledge/decisions/an-intention-is-an-amortised-deliberation.md.""" .

#################  The capability  #################

:IntentionCapability a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Intention capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "The family. Anything that keeps this agent's commitments is one of these, so a sibling may say 'commit to this' and ask what stands without knowing how commitments are kept." .

:Keeping a :IntentionCapability ; rdfs:label "Keeping" ;
    rdfs:comment "Keeps commitments by rule: a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one until it resolves or outlives the agent's patience. Granted by a STAKE AND A LEVER — acting for a subject that states needs, plus at least one way to act on them — because wanting without means and means without wants both leave nothing to commit to." .

#################  Where intentions are kept  #################
#
#  A graph of the agent's own, like its beliefs and its revisions, and NOT public: an intention
#  disclosed is strategy leaked. The BID is the public face of an intention to acquire — the
#  market sees what you do, never what you are trying to bring about. The graph is named from
#  the one identifier a process is given; see graphs.py.

#################  What this society tolerates  #################
#
#  Two figures ON THE FAMILY rather than on any agent: what counts as the world
#  answering, and how much unanswered evidence makes an affordance suspect. They
#  stayed here when the mind's STATES moved to the kernel, because a state is what a
#  mind contains while these are how THIS capability judges — the how, which is the
#  package's by rule 2.

:metFraction a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "met fraction" ;
    rdfs:comment "The fraction of an expected delta that counts as the world answering (#165). On the family, like suspectAfter: what this society accepts as evidence, not how the code is written. A quarter — generous to partially-absorbed doses, far above any instrument's grain. Chosen against the OTHER failure too: a margin so strict that honest doses read unmet would feed suspectAfter and mark truthful affordances false." .

:IntentionCapability :metFraction 0.25 .

:suspectAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "suspect after" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many consecutive unmet expectations for one (means, property) pair make the affordance suspect. On the family, like the patience bounds: a fact about what this society tolerates before it stops trusting a claim, not about how the code is written." .

:IntentionCapability :suspectAfter 3 .

#################  The commitment policy — a belief, bounded here  #################
#
#  How stubborn to be is an OPINION, so it is each agent's belief and review may move it. The
#  BOUNDS are constitutional and live on the family, exactly as sensing's cadence bounds do:
#  an agent that re-decides every second has retired the whole point of committing, and one that
#  never reconsiders will honour a dead commitment forever.

:patienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "patience seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one. Within it, a second impulse to do the same thing is ABSORBED — that is the amortisation, and with an LLM deliberator it is the cost model. Past it, a new adoption supersedes the old, which is recorded as dropped with the reason." .

:minPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minimum patience" .
:maxPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "maximum patience" .

:IntentionCapability :minPatienceS 10 ; :maxPatienceS 86400 .
